{% from 'ingressroute.yml.j2' import monitoring_route with context %}
---
{# monitoring_route, not ingressroute(): a LogQL client can't 302-dance through Authelia, so the
   ClientIP guard is the gate, as on every monitoring route. LAN-only by construction.

   The caller list this comment used to give — promtail's pusher, terraria-stats, monitor-bridge,
   homelab-mcp — was already wrong: all of them reach Loki on the ClusterIP
   (loki-homelab.<ns>.svc.cluster.local:3100), not through this route. As of 2026-08-24 the ONLY
   caller here is probe.py, which runs on a host rather than in a pod. See below. #}
{{ monitoring_route(
    container_item.name,
    container_item.hostname | default(container_item.name),
    container_item.port,
    '/loki/api/v1/',
    extra_client_cidrs=['10.42.0.1/32', '10.42.1.1/32']
  )
}}
{# DECIDED 2026-08-24: was PathPrefix(`/`) with extra_client_cidrs=['10.42.0.0/16'].

   Those two together gave EVERY POD the entire Loki API on an `auth_enabled: false` instance
   (configmap.yaml.j2). Not a theoretical reach — verified live from a source matching only the
   pod-CIDR clause: /config returned 200 (full config disclosure), /metrics 200,
   /loki/api/v1/push 405 (handler reached, so a POST would ingest) and /loki/api/v1/delete 400
   (param validation, so the delete handler is routed and the compactor has retention enabled).
   That is read of every log line the fleet emits, forged entries to poison the alert history
   `probe.py alerts` reconstructs from Loki, and a delete path.

   The macro's own contract said guard 2 should be "only the endpoint the check reads"; `/` was
   one of just two call sites of eight ignoring it, and both are fixed in this change.

   A NetworkPolicy could not have contained it. baseline-ingress admits `app=traefik` on all
   ports and the traefik policy's first ingress rule has no `from:`, so any pod may reach Traefik
   — and everything arriving through this route carries Traefik's identity, not the sender's.

   The two callers that used this route:
     - the Kuma "Loki" tile probed /ready, and is why the pod CIDR was admitted at all. It moved
       to ClusterIP (uptime-kuma/static-monitors.yaml.j2), where networkpolicy-loki-homelab
       admits it by podSelector. Everything else in-cluster — promtail, monitor-bridge,
       homelab-mcp, terraria/valheim-stats, Grafana, Prometheus — already used ClusterIP and is
       untouched.
     - probe.py runs on a HOST, not in a pod, so it genuinely needs the edge. It reaches only
       /loki/api/v1/labels and /loki/api/v1/query_range (scripts/diagnostics/probe_core.py:107,118), hence
       the prefix.

   The two /32s are the nodes' cni0 gateway addresses: host traffic to the VIP SNATs to them, so
   this is "the two cluster nodes" and nothing else. Same pair as netpol_baseline_node_cidrs,
   deliberately written out rather than referenced — that var is read by 16 workloads, and a
   future widening of it must not silently widen this route too.

   Residual, accepted: /loki/api/v1/ still contains push and delete, because PathPrefix cannot
   exclude. Those are now reachable only from the two nodes rather than from any pod. #}
